			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Inject user shell environment for GUI launches\n\nImplement a boot-time utility in `Source/Environment/Utility/EnhanceShellEnvironment` to resolve the minimal environment problem inherent in macOS/Linux GUI app launches (e.g., Finder, Dock, Spotlight).\n\nWhen launched non-interactively, the app receives a bare `PATH` missing user customizations like `NVM_DIR`, `HOMEBREW_PREFIX`, and `JAVA_HOME`. This previously caused child processes (Cocoon's Node, language servers, Git extensions) to fail or use outdated system binaries.\n\nThe solution spawns the user's login shell with `-ilc env` to capture their full runtime environment, then safely merges new variables into the parent process environment. It includes:\n- A 2-second timeout to prevent boot hangs from misbehaving `.rc` files.\n- A check to skip injection if already running from a TTY.\n- A safety filter (`IsPortableEnvName`) to reject non-standard variable names.\n- A strict policy to preserve existing environment variables unless they are unset, ensuring no overwrites of deliberate user overrides.\n\nThis ensures Mountain and its sidecars (Cocoon, Air) operate with the same environment context as a user's terminal session.",
			"SHA": "240102ddfe032c28ba28bd65c16ce7412642abf1"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:12:27Z",
			"File": [
				"⠁⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1212,
				16
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Introduce service registry and feature flag subsystem\n\nImplement the foundational runtime control plane within Mountain, consisting of a service discovery registry and a feature flag management system.\n\nIn `Source/IPC/Common/ServiceInfo`, define the data models for service lifecycle management: `ServiceState::Enum` (Running, Degraded, etc.), `ServicePerformance::Struct` (request/error counters with rolling mean latency), `ServiceEndpoint::Struct` (network/UDS metadata), and `ServiceInfo::Struct` (aggregated health state). The `ServiceRegistry::Struct` provides a thread-safe map for registering services, tracking heartbeat intervals, and filtering by health status (operational state, <30s heartbeat, <10% error rate).\n\nIn `Source/Telemetry/FeatureFlags`, refactor the existing monolithic module into a modular architecture adhering to the project's PascalCase naming convention. Extract `FeatureFlag::Struct`, `FlagCategory::Enum`, and `FeatureFlagError::Enum` into their own files. The `FeatureFlagRegistry::Struct` maintains a process-wide thread-safe registry (backed by `parking_lot::RwLock`) seeded with defaults like `ipc-compression` and `experimental-webgl`. Expose functional accessors (`IsEnabled::Fn`, `Enable::Fn`, `Disable::Fn`) via `GlobalRegistry` to allow the rest of the backend to gate experimental features without recompiling.\n\nThese additions enable the backend to dynamically monitor service health across the IPC landscape and toggle performance or experimental features at runtime.",
			"SHA": "1e9d527a0900769df28f60221d7282d7f3744a10"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
			"Date": "2026-05-02T09:07:10Z",
			"File": [
				"⠀⠀⠀⠀⠀",
				1196,
				4
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mountain): Define core IPC message types and priority system\n\nIntroduce the foundational data structures for the Inter-Process Communication layer within `Source/IPC/Common/MessageType`. This commit establishes the contract for request, response, and message handling across the Mountain backend and connected services.\n\nThe implementation adds:\n- `MessagePriority::Enum`: A four-tier priority ladder (Low, Normal, High, Critical) supporting comparison and ordering for scheduling logic.\n- `IPCCommand::Struct`: A builder-pattern command request allowing positional `Args`, named `Params`, and explicit priority assignment.\n- `IPCMessage::Struct`: A generic transport envelope including a UUID, timestamp, optional correlation ID for request tracking, and a priority field.\n- `IPCResponse::Struct`: A standardized response type with `Success`/`Error` constructors, preserving the correlation ID for async matching and capturing execution results or failure reasons.\n\nAll types derive `Serialize` and `Deserialize` to enable JSON transmission over the gRPC (Vine) protocol and Tauri IPC channels. These models unify the messaging interface, enabling the `Wind` service layer and `Cocoon` extension host to exchange structured data with explicit priority handling and traceability.",
			"SHA": "b95a7333e3b00c5c13850fdaeb0ad7e5ef0efa37"
		},
		{
			"Author": "Nikola Hristov",
